    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1015">

                <pb facs="0967" n="955" sameAs="frus:frus1943v04/pg955"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">890D.01/671: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Diplomatic Agent and Consul General at Beirut</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Wadsworth</hi>)
                        <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Beirut</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-01-25">January 25,
                        1943</date>.<lb/>[Received January 25—6:14 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>47. ZTN evening January 23, for publication yesterday’s newspapers, French
                    Delegation General issued following communiqué signed by Monsieur Helleu<anchor
                        n="8" xml:id="d1015-f1"/> “For The French National Committee”: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>The French National Committee,</p>
                        <p>Resolved to reinforce the independence of Syria and Lebanon, an
                            independence proclaimed in its name by General Catroux in 1941,<anchor
                                n="9" xml:id="d1015-f2"/>
                        </p>
                        <p>And considering, after consultation with the British Government, that the
                            evolution of the military situation in the country warrants
                            reestablishment of the constitutional regime,</p>
                        <p>Has given mandate to the Delegate General and Plenipotentiary,
                            Commander-in-Chief in the Levant, to take to this end all necessary
                            dispositions, after consultation with the Governments of the Syrian and
                            Lebanese Republics as well as with the principal local political
                            personalities.</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <p>The expected early return of General Catroux to the Levant will thus mark a
                    decisive step in the political life of Lebanon and Syria.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Wadsworth</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1015-f1" n="8" xml:id="d1015-f1-content">
                <p>Jean Helleu, Acting Free French Delegate in Syria and Lebanon, in the absence of
                    General Catroux.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1015-f2" n="9" xml:id="d1015-f2-content">
                <p>For correspondence regarding this subject, see <ref target="frus1941v03#pg_785">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1941, vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">iii</hi>, pp. 785</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
